@endnode

@node Intro "Introduction"

@{b}Introduction@{ub}

Velox is a web browser for classic Amigas: AmigaOS 3.1 or newer on a 68020 or better. All of it is written in 68k assembly, and it decodes JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP and SVG images itself, so it needs no datatypes for them.

Velox stays responsive while it loads: the page scrolls and links, gadgets and menus work while pages and their images still come in. A page whose style sheets must still be fetched is shown plain as it comes, and laid out with them once they are there. Pages can load in several @{" tabs " link Tabs} at once.

It shows the web of today as far as an Amiga can: HTML with a tolerant parser, much of CSS, tables, forms, frames, image maps, cookies and https. There is no JavaScript; see @{" Limits " link Limits}.

@endnode

@node Requirements "Requirements"

@{b}Requirements@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 3.1 or newer
  - A 68020 or better (68030, 68040, 68060, an emulated CPU)
  - A TCP/IP stack with bsdsocket.library: Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami...
  - For https: AmiSSL 6, or velox-proxy.py running on another computer (see @{" https and the proxy " link Https})
  - Fast RAM: pages, their images and the pages kept for Back live there. With Chip RAM alone, as on a stock A1200, few pages fit. A few MB will do without https; with AmiSSL, 8 MB or more (AmiSSL alone takes about 4 MB)

Nothing more is needed for images: Velox decodes JPEG, PNG, GIF and WebP and draws SVG itself. Other formats (ILBM, BMP...) go through the picture datatypes the system has.

@endnode

@node Installing "Installing"

@{b}Installing@{ub}

Copy the Velox drawer anywhere, for instance to Work: or SYS:Internet. It holds:

  Velox            the program
  Velox.guide      this guide
  README.md        the same in short, as text
  LICENSE          the GNU General Public License 3
  velox-proxy.py   the proxy, for a computer on your network

Velox makes a Cache drawer beside itself for its disk cache. Its settings live in ENV:Velox and ENVARC:Velox. To uninstall, delete the drawer and ENVARC:Velox.

@endnode

@node Starting "Starting"

@{b}Starting@{ub}

From Workbench, double-click the Velox icon. Its tool types are the @{" settings " link Settings}; a disabled one is in parentheses, so remove them to use it.

From a Shell:

  Velox [URL] [PROXY=host:port] [LOG=file]

  URL      the page to open (http://, https://, file:, about:velox)
  PROXY    send all requests through a proxy
  LOG      write a log of what Velox does to a file (for finding bugs)

Two more are for the Shell alone: DUMP prints what the server sends for the URL (a page's HTML), INFO its status and headers, without opening a window.

The window opens where it was last closed. Ctrl-C in the Shell stops what loads, or quits when nothing does.

@endnode

@node Settings "Settings"

@{b}Settings@{ub}

A setting is a tool type in Velox's icon or an environment variable, set with SetEnv; SAVE keeps it:

  SetEnv SAVE Velox/Home http://frogfind.com/

  Tool type           Variable           Meaning
  PROXY=host:port     Velox/Proxy        all requests through a proxy
  HOME=url            Velox/Home         the home page (and start page)
  URL=url                                the page to start with
  DOWNLOADDIR=drawer  Velox/DownloadDir  where downloads go (RAM:)
  NOIMAGES            Velox/NoImages     load no images
  PUBSCREEN=name      Velox/PubScreen    open on that public screen
                      Velox/Language     languages asked for (en; sv,en;q=0.8)
                      Velox/CacheDir     the disk cache (PROGDIR:Cache)
                      Velox/CacheSize    its size in KB (16384; 0: none)
                      Velox/KeepPages    pages kept for Back (4; 0: none)
                      Velox/TlsCiphers   TLS 1.3 cipher suites
                      Velox/Tls13        TLS 1.3 wherever servers have it
                      Velox/InsecureTLS  no certificate checks (testing)

Without a home page, Home goes to about:velox.

@endnode

@node Window "The window"

@{b}The window@{ub}

At the top, the @{b}toolbar@{ub}: Back, Forward, Reload (it stops a page while it loads) and Home, and the address field. Type an address and press Return; "amiga.org" is enough.

As you type, a list under the address field offers the pages you visited that match: those beginning with what you typed first (http://, https:// and www. do not count), then those that have it anywhere, the latest first. Down and Up choose one, Return goes there, and so does a click; Esc closes the list. Typing never waits for the list: it follows when there is time.

The @{b}tab strip@{ub} lies below it: each tab's page icon (the site's favicon) and title, a small mark while it loads, a close box, and "+" for a new tab. See @{" Tabs " link Tabs}.

The @{b}page@{ub} fills the rest, with its scroller at the right. A page of frames shows its frames side by side or stacked, each scrolling on its own, with a scrollbar of its own where it needs one.

At the bottom, the @{b}status line@{ub}: the address of the link under the mouse, what loads and how far it has come, and a progress bar at the right.

@endnode

@node Keys "Keys"

@{b}Keys@{ub}

  Ctrl-L               the address field: the address stays, the
                       cursor after it (the home page's is emptied)
  Tab, Shift-Tab       next, previous link or form field
                       (a text field: ready for typing)
  Return               follow the link, use the control
                       (in a text field: send the form)
  Shift-Return         open the focused link in a tab behind
  Cursor up, down      scroll a line (with Shift: a page)
  Space                a page down
  Ctrl-Up, Ctrl-Down   the top, the bottom of the page
  Backspace, Ctrl-B    back
  Ctrl-F               forward
  Ctrl-R               reload
  Esc                  stop loading (the page, or its images)
  Ctrl-H               home
  /                    find in the page (Return: next, Esc: done)
  Ctrl-D               add a bookmark
  Ctrl-K               the bookmarks
  Amiga-S              save the page or file
  Amiga-C              copy the address (of the focused link)
  Amiga-U              view the page's source
  Amiga-T              a new tab: the home page, the address
                       field ready for typing
  Amiga-W              close the tab
  Amiga-.  Amiga-,     the next, the previous tab
  Ctrl-Q               quit

The Amiga key works in place of Ctrl for the menu commands. Keys typed while a page loads are not lost.

@{b}Editing text@{ub}

In a text field or a text area, the keys work as in a notepad. A text area wraps its lines at the blanks that fit.

  Cursor keys          a character, a line up or down
  Ctrl-Left, -Right    the line's start, its end
  Ctrl-Up, -Down       the text's start, its end
  Shift with them      select
  Ctrl-A               select all
  Ctrl-C               copy the selection
  Ctrl-X               cut it
  Ctrl-V               paste
  Ctrl-Z               undo (as many steps as you like)
  Ctrl-Y               redo (also Shift-Ctrl-Z)
  Backspace, Del       delete (the selection, if there is one)
  Return               a new line (in a field: send the form)
  Esc                  stop editing
  Tab                  on to the next field

Amiga works in place of Ctrl here too. Typing is undone a word at a time.

@endnode

@node Mouse "The mouse"

@{b}The mouse@{ub}

A click on a link follows it; the status line shows where it goes. Shift-click, or the middle button, opens it in a tab behind the one shown.

Drag over text to select it: it is copied to the clipboard at once.

In a text field or text area, a click puts the cursor where you click, a Shift-click selects from the cursor to there, and dragging selects.

In the tab strip, a click shows a tab, a click on its close box or the middle button closes it, and "+" opens a new one.

The mouse wheel scrolls the page, or the frame under it.

@endnode

@node Tabs "Tabs"

@{b}Tabs@{ub}

Each tab is a page of its own, with its own history for Back and Forward, and all tabs load at once: a tab behind gets its page, style sheets and images while you read another. The tab shown always comes first, so tabs behind never make it slower to use.

  - Amiga-T or "+" opens a tab with the home page.
  - Shift-click, the middle button or Shift-Return opens a link in a tab behind.
  - A link or form with target="_blank" opens in a new tab; one with a target name opens in the tab of that name, which later links with the same target use too.
  - Amiga-. and Amiga-, show the next and the previous tab; Amiga-W or a close box closes one.

A tab behind that loads nothing gives its memory for loading back; when memory runs short, the pages kept for Back in any tab go first.

@endnode

@node Bookmarks "Bookmarks and more"

@{b}Bookmarks@{ub}

Ctrl-D adds the page shown to ENVARC:Velox/Bookmarks.html and Ctrl-K shows them. It is an ordinary page: edit it as you like.

@{b}Pages visited@{ub}

The last 500 pages visited are kept in ENVARC:Velox/Visited, for the list under the address field. Delete that file to forget them.

@{b}Cookies@{ub}

Velox keeps cookies, and saves those a site wants kept (with an expiry date) in ENVARC:Velox/Cookies. Delete that file to forget them.

@{b}Downloads@{ub}

Anything that is not a page (an archive, a picture opened on its own...) is downloaded; a file requester asks where it goes (DOWNLOADDIR first). Amiga-S saves a page or file shown.

@{b}Caches@{ub}

Pages left are kept in memory for Back and Forward (Velox/KeepPages) and come back at once, as they were. Pages, style sheets and images are also kept on disk (Velox/CacheDir), laid out pages too, so a page seen before comes back fast even after Velox was quit. Reload fetches everything anew.

@endnode

@node Https "https and the proxy"

@{b}https@{ub}

With AmiSSL 6 installed, Velox opens https pages itself and checks the servers' certificates. The cryptography runs in a process of its own, so the window stays usable meanwhile. On a 68020 a first connection to a server takes several seconds (over 30 for a server whose certificates are all of the slow ECDSA P-384 kind, the first time: Velox remembers certificates it has checked); a connection resumed later takes a fraction of a second.

@{b}The proxy@{ub}

Without AmiSSL, or to make https fast on a slow Amiga, run velox-proxy.py on a modern computer on your network (Python 3; see the comment at its top) and set PROXY= or Velox/Proxy to that computer and port. The proxy does the encryption and keeps its connections to servers alive.

@endnode

@node Features "What Velox shows"

@{b}What Velox shows@{ub}

  - HTML with a tolerant parser; UTF-8, Latin-1 and Windows-1252 pages
  - CSS: selectors with specificity (also :not(), :is(), attributes, structural ones), colours (also rgb(), hsl() and calc() in them), custom properties with var(), flexbox (rows, columns, wrapping, grow, gaps, alignment, order), CSS grid (column templates with fr, minmax() and repeat(), named areas, lines and spans, gaps), animated GIFs, <blink> and <marquee> (animated without flicker: only they are painted again), CSS tables (display: table and table-cell on any element), image sizes from CSS (width, height, max-width, max-height), borders (drawn solid, one colour per box), table heights, inline-block and inline-flex boxes, floated elements side by side, fonts, margins, padding, widths, alignment, display, floats, media queries, backgrounds; links styled as buttons look like buttons
  - Tables, lists, preformatted text, headings, rules
  - Images: JPEG (also progressive), PNG (also with transparency), GIF and WebP (lossy and lossless) decoded by Velox, SVG drawn by it, dithered to the screen's colours; other formats through datatypes; background images
  - Forms: text fields, passwords, check boxes, radio buttons, selects, text areas, files to send (chosen with a file requester); GET and POST (also multipart/form-data, for uploads)
  - Frames, each scrolling on its own; links into a frame load that frame only
  - Image maps, client and server side
  - http and https, keep-alive, redirects, chunked transfers, cookies, pages with a password (http://user:password@host/)
  - <meta> refresh, find in page, view source, local file: pages

@endnode

@node Limits "Limits"

@{b}Limits@{ub}

  - No JavaScript: pages that need it to show anything stay empty or show what they have for browsers without it.
  - Iframes are shown as links to their pages.
  - Fonts: one proportional font (helvetica) and one fixed one (courier), in six sizes; font-family picks one of the two.
  - Absolute positioning and grid are laid out as ordinary blocks.

@endnode

